  <section>
    <h2>Fact Check: Common claims about rent regulation</h2>

    <div class="callout">
      <h3>"Rent control always reduces housing supply."</h3>
      <p><strong>Not the modern kind.</strong> The University of Minnesota's 2021 <a href="https://www.cura.umn.edu/sites/cura.umn.edu/files/2021-08/Minneapolis-Rent-Stabilization-Study-web.pdf">Minneapolis Rent Stabilization Study</a> found <em>"little empirical evidence that rent control policies negatively impact new construction."</em> Most programs exempt new buildings, and construction depends far more on credit markets and local cycles than on rent caps. A CPI + 3% cap still lets landlords earn 90th-percentile returns. See <a href="/everywhere-else">Everywhere Else</a> for examples.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <h3>"Rent control is the same as a rent freeze."</h3>
      <p><strong>No.</strong> A rent <em>freeze</em> (0% increases for a set period) is one specific policy. Modern rent <em>stabilization</em> allows annual increases tied to inflation or operating costs — typically 3–7%. Zohran Mamdani's 2026 freeze is the first-ever two-year freeze in NYC history. See the <a href="/mamdani-plan">NYC Rent Freeze</a> page for the full details.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <h3>"What about San Francisco? Didn't rent control backfire there?"</h3>
      <p><strong>That study studied a different policy.</strong> The 2019 Diamond/McQuade/Qian paper looked at San Francisco's 1994 expansion of a <em>first-generation hard-cap</em> system that applied to all units, including new construction. No modern proposal replicates that design. The paper itself notes its findings don't generalize to second-generation rent stabilization.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <h3>"Won't this hurt small landlords?"</h3>
      <p><strong>Most programs exempt small buildings.</strong> California's AB 1482 exempts single-family homes and owner-occupied duplexes. Many local ordinances have owner-occupancy carve-outs. The regulations are designed to target corporate landlords and large portfolios, not mom-and-pop owners.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <h3>"If the private market won't build affordable housing, what's the alternative?"</h3>
      <p><strong>Public and community development.</strong> Mamdani's <a href="/block-by-block">Block by Block</a> plan creates a municipal development corporation to build directly on city-owned land. Community land trusts in <a href="/everywhere-else">Hawaii, Seattle, and across the country</a> have been building permanently affordable homes for decades — outside the private market entirely. When private developers won't deliver, public institutions can.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
